        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How to Seal Pavers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-to-seal-pavers/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you seal pavers step by step?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pull weeds and moss, wash the surface at moderate pressure, treat any oil or rust stains specifically, then allow 24 to 48 hours of dry weather. Install and cure polymeric sand if the joints need it. Then apply two thin coats at 50 to 90 degrees out of direct sun, respecting the recoat window, and keep off it for 24 hours."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should pavers dry before sealing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "At least 24 to 48 hours of dry weather after washing, and longer for shaded, north-facing or heavily shaded patios, or in humid conditions. Tape a square of clear plastic to the surface for a few hours: condensation underneath means moisture is still coming out and the pavers are not ready."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you seal new pavers right away?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. New concrete pavers push soluble salts to the surface for months as they cure, appearing as white efflorescence. Sealing over that traps the salts under the film and produces a permanent white bloom that can only be fixed by stripping. Wait 60 to 90 days, wash off the efflorescence, then seal."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you put polymeric sand down before or after sealing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Before, and it must be fully cured first. Sweep it in, compact it, activate it with water per the instructions, then allow at least 24 hours before sealing, checking your specific product since some want longer. Sealing over uncured sand traps moisture in the joints and stops the polymer bonding."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I spray or roll paver sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The best finish usually comes from spraying and then immediately backrolling, which avoids both the overlap striping of spraying alone and the lap lines and bubbles of rolling alone. Use a short-nap roller, 1/4 or 3/8 inch, and a solvent-resistant sprayer if the sealer is solvent based."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many coats of paver sealer do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Two thin coats, essentially always. One thick coat traps vapour in the film and produces the milky blush people describe as the sealer turning white, and it is less durable and less rich-looking than two thin ones. Respect the recoat window on the tin, which is often one to four hours."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long before you can walk on sealed pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Light foot traffic after about 24 hours, furniture after 48, and vehicles after 72 hours minimum on a driveway, with all of those extended in cool or humid weather. Keep sprinklers off the area and pets away for the same period, since paw prints and water spotting in a curing film are permanent."
