          <a href="#what-goes-wrong" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. What Goes Wrong</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          This is a job where the part that takes the longest is waiting, and the part people compress is also the waiting. Almost every cloudy, blotchy, peeling paver patio you have seen was sealed a day too early.
        </p>

        <div id="prerequisites" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Three Things That Must Be True Before You Open the Tin
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check these first, because any one of them being false turns a good sealer into a surface you will be stripping off next spring.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Pavers Are Old Enough</h3>

          <p>
            New concrete pavers need to weather before sealing, typically 60 to 90 days and sometimes longer. Fresh concrete pushes soluble salts to the surface as it cures, which appears as a white haze called efflorescence. Seal over that process and you trap the salts under the film, where they produce a permanent white bloom that can only be fixed by stripping the sealer. Let the pavers go through a full wet-and-dry cycle or two, wash off the efflorescence, and then seal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">They Are Genuinely Dry</h3>

          <p>
            This is the one that catches everyone. Pavers are porous and hold water well after the surface looks dry. After washing, allow 24 to 48 hours of dry weather, and longer in humid conditions or if the patio is shaded. Check the forecast too: rain within 24 hours of application can ruin a fresh coat. The classic test is to tape a square of clear plastic to the surface for a few hours; condensation underneath means there is still moisture coming out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Weather Will Cooperate</h3>

          <p>
            Most sealers want 50 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit and no direct hot sun on the surface during application. Hot pavers flash the solvent or water off before the sealer penetrates, which leaves it sitting on top as a film that blushes, and in the worst case sets as a visible cloudy layer. Work in the morning or on an overcast day, following the shade around the patio if you have to.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE THAT PREVENTS MOST FAILURES</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Dry pavers, moderate temperature, no direct sun, two thin coats.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Cloudiness, blushing and peeling almost always trace back to moisture trapped under the sealer or to a coat applied too thick or too hot. None of those is a product defect and none costs anything to avoid — they just cost patience.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cleaning, and the Wait That Follows It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer locks in whatever is on the surface, permanently and under a film. Every stain, every patch of moss and every bit of dried mortar you leave becomes a feature of the patio for the life of the sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by pulling weeds from the joints and scraping out any moss. Then wash the surface. A pressure washer is the usual tool, but keep it moderate: a wide 25 or 40 degree tip at around 1,200 to 1,500 PSI held well back is enough. Excessive pressure blasts jointing sand out, etches the paver face, and on older pavers can expose aggregate, all of which show through the sealer afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat stains specifically rather than hoping the wash takes them. Organic staining from leaves and moss responds to a percarbonate cleaner. Rust needs an oxalic acid product. Oil needs a degreaser or poultice, and oil is the one that most commonly ruins a sealing job because a sealer will not bond over it. Our guide on removing oil stains covers the approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            If there is existing sealer on the pavers, you have a decision to make. A compatible water-based sealer can often be recoated over a sound existing water-based film, but solvent over water, or any sealer over a degraded flaky film, will fail. If the existing coating is peeling, hazy or unknown, strip it. Recoating over a failing film guarantees a failing result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then wait. Twenty-four to 48 hours of dry weather is the minimum after washing, and shaded or north-facing patios need longer. This waiting period is the single most compressed step in the whole job, usually because a free weekend has two days in it and the job feels like it should fit. It does not; plan the wash for one weekend and the seal for the next if the weather is at all marginal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sand-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Polymeric Sand Goes in Before the Sealer
          </h2>

          <p>
            The order here confuses people, and getting it wrong means either sand that will not harden or a sealer that cannot reach the joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Joint sand goes in first, and it must be fully set before the sealer goes on. Polymeric sand needs to be swept in, compacted, and then activated with water according to its instructions, after which it needs to cure. Sealing over sand that has not cured traps moisture in the joints and prevents the polymer bonding, giving you soft sand under a sealed surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allow at least 24 hours after activating polymeric sand before sealing, and check the specific product, since some want considerably longer. Our guide on how long after polymeric sand you can seal pavers covers the intervals in detail, and the related question of whether to seal before or after sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make sure the sand surface is swept clean and at the right height before you seal. Polymeric sand should sit slightly below the paver chamfer, not level with the top, and any haze left on the paver faces must come off before sealing. Polymeric sand haze locked under a sealer is one of the more miserable problems to fix, and it is covered in our guide on removing it.
          </p>

          <p>
            One alternative worth knowing about: some sealers are specifically formulated as joint stabilising sealers, designed to soak into ordinary sand and bind it. Those are applied to regular jointing sand rather than polymeric, and they follow a different sequence. If you are using one, follow its instructions rather than this section, because the whole point is that the sealer is doing the sand's job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not seal a patio with open or washed-out joints on the theory that the sealer will fill them. It will not, and it will run down into the gaps, wasting product and potentially pooling where it dries glossy and uneven.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="method" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sprayer, Roller, or Both
          </h2>

          <p>
            Application method affects the finish more than most people expect, and the right answer depends on the sealer type and the paver texture.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch Out For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pump sprayer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Even coverage, fast, textured pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Overlap marks, wind drift</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Roller</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Control, small areas, smooth pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bubbles, lap lines, roller fluff</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spray then backroll</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best overall finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Requires two passes, more time</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brush</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Edges and awkward corners only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Far too slow for a full patio</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For most patios the best result comes from spraying and then backrolling: spray an even wet coat, then immediately go over it with a roller to spread it uniformly and push it into the surface texture. That combination avoids both the overlap striping that spraying alone can leave and the lap lines and bubbles a roller alone tends to produce.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the sprayer against the sealer. Solvent-based sealers destroy the seals in an ordinary garden pump sprayer within one use, so you need a solvent-resistant sprayer with Viton seals. This is an inexpensive part and a genuinely common frustration, because the sprayer fails partway through the patio.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a roller with a short nap, typically 1/4 or 3/8 inch, and one rated for solvents if you are using a solvent sealer. A long-nap roller holds too much product and applies it too thickly, which is the main cause of a cloudy finish. And avoid cheap rollers that shed, since the fibres become a permanent part of the sealed surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, work out your exit route before you start. Sealing yourself into a corner of a patio is a genuinely common and entirely avoidable mistake, and stepping on a freshly sealed surface leaves marks that will not blend out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Application: Two Thin Coats, Always
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the section to follow precisely, because the failure modes here are permanent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply two thin coats rather than one heavy one. This is the most important technique rule in paver sealing. A thick coat cannot release its solvent or water from the bottom up, so moisture and vapour get trapped in the film and produce the milky, cloudy blush that people describe as the sealer turning white. Two thin coats give a better wet look, better durability and a far lower chance of failure than one heavy coat, which gives none of those things.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage varies with paver porosity, typically 100 to 200 square feet per gallon per coat, with rough or old pavers at the thirsty end. Your first coat will always go further than expected on tight pavers and disappear alarmingly fast on porous ones. Our coverage guidance is worth reading before you buy so you are not making a second trip mid-job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect the recoat window. Most sealers specify a time between coats, often in the region of one to four hours, and it matters in both directions: too soon and you trap solvent under the second coat, too late and the first coat has cured enough that the second sits on rather than merges with it. Read the tin for your specific product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a wet edge and work in manageable sections. Sealer that has begun to set before you overlap it leaves a visible line that will not blend, so plan strips across the patio and complete each one before stopping. On a large patio, two people is genuinely easier than one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not puddle it. Any low spot where sealer pools will dry glossier, darker and often cloudier than the surrounding area, and once cured that difference is permanent. If you see a puddle forming, spread it out with the roller immediately rather than leaving it to soak in.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Thin coats, never one thick</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 h</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dry time before sealing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100-200</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon per coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50-90°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Application temperature</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="curing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cure Time and Getting Back on the Patio
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drying and curing are different things, and the gap between them is where damage happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            The surface is usually dry to the touch within a few hours, but the film continues to harden for considerably longer. Typical guidance is foot traffic after 24 hours, furniture after 48, and vehicles after 72 hours or longer on a driveway. In cool or humid weather extend all of those.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Activity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Wait</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light foot traffic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Film still soft, marks easily</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Replacing furniture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feet will dent an uncured film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vehicles on a driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72 hours minimum</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tyres lift a soft film, especially turning</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full chemical cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-30 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Product dependent; avoid harsh cleaners</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Keep the patio dry through the initial cure. Rain on a sealer that has not set can cause blushing, and sprinklers are an easily forgotten culprit: turn off any irrigation that reaches the patio for a few days, because a timer running at 6am onto a patio sealed the previous afternoon will ruin it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep pets off it for the same period. Paw prints in a curing sealer are permanent, and some sealers remain slightly tacky for a day in a way that is unpleasant for an animal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not put pots or planters straight back onto a new surface even after it is walkable. A pot that traps moisture against a curing film leaves a ring, and on a wet look sealer that ring will be visible. Give it the full week before anything sits permanently on the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing to expect and not panic about: a solvent-based wet look sealer often looks alarmingly glossy and uneven for the first day or two as it levels and cures. Judge the finish after a week, not on the evening you applied it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-goes-wrong" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Goes Wrong, and Whether It Is Fixable
          </h2>

          <p>
            The four common failures, in order of how often they happen.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cloudy or Milky Finish</h3>

          <p>
            Almost always trapped moisture or a coat applied too thick. Sometimes it resolves as the film finishes releasing moisture over a few warm dry days. If it does not, a solvent-based sealer can sometimes be re-solvated by applying xylene to re-open the film and let the moisture out; a water-based one usually has to be stripped. Our guide on why paver sealer turns white covers diagnosis and the fixes in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Peeling or Flaking</h3>

          <p>
            The sealer did not bond, generally because the surface was dirty, oily, still sealed with something incompatible, or damp. There is no fix short of stripping and starting again, which is why the cleaning and drying steps are worth the patience.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blotchy or Uneven Sheen</h3>

          <p>
            Uneven application, puddling in low spots, or pavers with genuinely different porosity absorbing different amounts. A second light coat will often even it out if the problem is absorption. If it is puddling, the thick areas may need spot attention.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slippery When Wet</h3>

          <p>
            A predictable consequence of a gloss or wet look sealer rather than a defect, and it matters around pools and on steps and slopes. It is preventable with an anti-slip additive mixed into the final coat, and much harder to solve afterwards. Our guide on whether sealing makes pavers slippery covers the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            The theme across all four is that prevention is cheap and remediation usually means stripping. Given that stripping a sealed patio is a considerably worse job than sealing one, the waiting and the two-thin-coats discipline are the best value in the entire project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Pavers That Are Not Fully Dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pavers are porous and hold water long after the surface looks dry. Allow 24 to 48 hours of dry weather after washing, longer if shaded or humid, and check no rain is forecast for 24 hours after. Trapped moisture under the film is the cause of most cloudy, milky finishes, and it usually means stripping to fix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying One Thick Coat Instead of Two Thin Ones</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat cannot release its solvent or water from the bottom up, so vapour is trapped in the film and it blushes white. Two thin coats give better depth, better durability and a far lower failure rate. This is the single most important technique rule in paver sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing New Pavers Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh concrete pushes soluble salts to the surface for months, appearing as a white efflorescence haze. Sealing over that traps the salts under the film permanently. Wait 60 to 90 days, let the pavers go through wet and dry cycles, wash the efflorescence off, and then seal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working in Direct Hot Sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hot pavers flash off the carrier before the sealer penetrates, leaving it as a surface film that blushes and can cure visibly cloudy. Apply at 50 to 90 degrees on an overcast day or in the morning, following the shade around the patio if necessary.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Standard Pump Sprayer With Solvent Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent-based sealers destroy the seals in an ordinary garden sprayer, often partway through the patio, which leaves you with a half-sealed surface and a set edge you cannot blend into. Use a solvent-resistant sprayer with Viton seals; it is an inexpensive part.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you seal pavers step by step?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pull weeds and moss, wash the surface at moderate pressure, treat any oil or rust stains specifically, then allow 24 to 48 hours of dry weather. Install and cure polymeric sand if the joints need it. Then apply two thin coats at 50 to 90 degrees out of direct sun, respecting the recoat window, and keep off it for 24 hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should pavers dry before sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 24 to 48 hours of dry weather after washing, and longer for shaded, north-facing or heavily shaded patios, or in humid conditions. Tape a square of clear plastic to the surface for a few hours: condensation underneath means moisture is still coming out and the pavers are not ready.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal new pavers right away?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. New concrete pavers push soluble salts to the surface for months as they cure, appearing as white efflorescence. Sealing over that traps the salts under the film and produces a permanent white bloom that can only be fixed by stripping. Wait 60 to 90 days, wash off the efflorescence, then seal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you put polymeric sand down before or after sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Before, and it must be fully cured first. Sweep it in, compact it, activate it with water per the instructions, then allow at least 24 hours before sealing, checking your specific product since some want longer. Sealing over uncured sand traps moisture in the joints and stops the polymer bonding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I spray or roll paver sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best finish usually comes from spraying and then immediately backrolling, which avoids both the overlap striping of spraying alone and the lap lines and bubbles of rolling alone. Use a short-nap roller, 1/4 or 3/8 inch, and a solvent-resistant sprayer if the sealer is solvent based.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of paver sealer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two thin coats, essentially always. One thick coat traps vapour in the film and produces the milky blush people describe as the sealer turning white, and it is less durable and less rich-looking than two thin ones. Respect the recoat window on the tin, which is often one to four hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before you can walk on sealed pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Light foot traffic after about 24 hours, furniture after 48, and vehicles after 72 hours minimum on a driveway, with all of those extended in cool or humid weather. Keep sprinklers off the area and pets away for the same period, since paw prints and water spotting in a curing film are permanent.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

