        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The sealer is rarely the variable. Dry pavers, moderate temperature, no direct sun, cured joint sand, and two thin coats instead of one thick one will produce a good result with almost any decent product. Reverse any one of those and the best sealer on the market will blush, peel or dry blotchy.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The step people compress is the waiting, because a wash and a seal feel like they should fit into one weekend. They usually do not. Wash one weekend and seal the next if the weather is at all marginal, and keep sprinklers and pets off the patio for the first couple of days. Stripping a failed sealer is a far worse job than waiting an extra day to apply it properly.
          </p>
        </div>

